    "plan_strategy": "Attempt 1: replace the linear baseline with a gradient-boosted tree ensemble to improve the ranking itself, and move the operating point at the same time with an explicit class weight. Fit hist_gbdt with max_iter=400, learning_rate=0.06, max_leaf_nodes=63, min_samples_leaf=40, l2_regularization=1.0, early_stopping=True with validation_fraction=0.1 and n_iter_no_change=30 so the iteration count self-limits inside the 600 s budget. Because hist_gbdt has no class_weight and the executor's only imbalance lever for it is a weight map, pass class_weight={\"0\": 1, \"1\": 6} (slightly below the 7.55 frequency ratio) to pull the default 0.5 cut toward higher recall without collapsing specificity. Preprocessing: impute median, scale false (trees are scale-invariant), no missingness columns — the card reports 0% numeric missingness and the 'unknown' categories are real one-hot levels the model already sees, so those columns would add nothing. Read on return: roc_auc / pr_auc / balanced_accuracy_at_best_cut versus the baseline's 0.9101 / 0.5557 / 0.84 to see whether the ranking actually moved, and cut_headroom plus the recall-vs-specificity pair to see whether the weight 6 landed near the optimal cut. If cut_headroom is small and balanced_accuracy_at_best_cut is above ~0.84, the next attempt tunes the weight; if the ceiling itself did not rise, the next attempt swaps family (xgboost or random_forest) rather than reweighting."
